  "body": "This PR takes the pin test that the agent of mkinney wrote for #3554 (branch `mkinney:issue/3554-tilted-hole-pin`, commit `418e88034`). The coordinator opens it; the commit and its author do not change.\n\n**What #3554 asked:** check if a hole that is tilted some degrees from the tool axis is recognized as a Z hole and drilled along Z. That would be a wrong cut.\n\n**Result (issue comment of 2026-11-22T19:29Z):** it is not. The circle fit in `classify` (`recognize.rs`) uses the wall vertices of the top and of the bottom of the hole. A tilt of 2 degrees over 10 mm moves the section centre by 0.35 mm, the fit residual is near 0.17 mm, and `FIT_TOL` (0.02 mm) refuses the wall. A short tilted hole can pass; then `FIT_TOL` limits the wall movement to 0.02 mm, the same tolerance as each tessellated hole.\n\n**The tests** (`crates/motion/src/drilling/tests_tilted.rs`):\n- `tilted_wall_builder_at_zero_tilt_is_a_z_hole`: the fixture at 0 degrees gives one Z hole.\n- `tilted_holes_in_a_plate_are_not_drilled_along_z`: a 10 mm plate with holes tilted 2 and 5 degrees (also -2 degrees, and 1, 2 and 8 bands): no Z hole and no cross hole.\n- `a_tilt_the_fit_accepts_moves_no_wall_past_fit_tol`: thin walls with a small tilt; where the fit accepts one, each wall vertex is within `FIT_TOL` of the circle.\n\nMutation by the author: with `FIT_TOL` set to 0.5 the second test fails; with 0.02 it passes.\n\n**Not in this PR:** a tilted hole is dropped with no named message. A named skip is an enhancement, not a defect.\n\nNo product code changes. `Changelog: none`\n\nFixes #3554\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
